# This repository is developed on Windows, where core.autocrlf=true converts
# LF to CRLF on checkout. Three kinds of file must not be converted, because
# they take part in byte-for-byte comparisons against freshly generated
# content:
#
#   src/**/*.js       Rollup can carry line endings from source segments into
#                     the bundle, so a CRLF working copy of the sources would
#                     produce a CRLF bundle in memory.
#   dist/*.js         `npm run verify:dist` rebuilds the bundle in memory and
#                     compares it with the committed artifact.
#   test/baseline/**  the characterization baselines are written and re-read by
#                     Node with LF newlines (see test/helpers/characterization.js).
#
# There are two independent ways line endings could diverge, which is why
# sources and artifact are pinned TOGETHER rather than one of them:
#
#   1. The separator lines Rollup generates itself are normally LF, but the
#      code between them comes from the source files, so a CRLF working copy of
#      src/ yields a bundle that mixes both.
#   2. Even with LF sources, Git could convert the committed artifact on
#      checkout, so the file on disk would no longer match what a rebuild
#      produces in memory.
#
# Pinning only one side would leave the other free to drift and reintroduce
# exactly the mismatch this is meant to prevent. verify-dist detects a
# remaining case symmetrically and reports it as a checkout problem rather than
# a stale bundle.
#
# Everything else keeps the repository's normal handling.
src/**/*.js text eol=lf
dist/*.js text eol=lf
test/baseline/** text eol=lf

# The characterization baselines are verbatim recordings of what the card
# actually emits, not hand-written files. The card's template literals do
# produce trailing whitespace inside the generated markup and CSS, so
# `git diff --check` would flag ~1500 lines in them on every change. Stripping
# that whitespace would corrupt the golden masters — the whole point is that
# they match the real output byte for byte. Exempting them keeps the whitespace
# check meaningful for the sources it is actually there to protect.
test/baseline/** -whitespace
